# Git workflow

After a coherent chunk of work is finished, commit it and push to the remote. Do this without waiting to be asked. Skip both when the user explicitly says not to commit.

- One commit per coherent change. Do not batch unrelated work.
- Write a 1–2 sentence message that states why the change was made.
- Follow the repository's existing message style.
- Never commit secrets (`.env`, credentials, keys). Warn the user if they ask to commit those files.
- Never update git config, skip hooks, or force-push unless the user explicitly asks.
- If a commit hook fails, fix the problem and make a new commit.
